1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,412
How the Body Heals a Cut.

2
00:00:02,362 --> 00:00:10,750
The human body's ability to repair a cut or wound is a remarkable biological process involving four precisely orchestrated phases.

3
00:00:10,750 --> 00:00:27,337
According to a comprehensive review in Nature Reviews Molecular Cell Biology, wound healing is one of the most complex cascades in human physiology, requiring the coordinated action of multiple cell types, signaling molecules, and extracellular matrix components.

4
00:00:27,337 --> 00:00:31,587
The process begins immediately after injury with hemostasis.

5
00:00:31,587 --> 00:00:37,962
Blood vessels constrict to limit bleeding, and platelets aggregate at the site to form a temporary clot.

6
00:00:37,962 --> 00:00:45,125
This clot releases cytokines and growth factors that act as chemical signals, recruiting immune cells to the wound.

7
00:00:45,125 --> 00:00:50,187
The second phase, inflammation, typically lasts two to five days.

8
00:00:50,187 --> 00:00:59,887
Neutrophils arrive first to destroy bacteria and remove debris, followed by macrophages that clear dead cells and secrete factors essential for tissue repair.

9
00:00:59,887 --> 00:01:12,025
Research from the NIH National Institute of General Medical Sciences emphasizes that this inflammatory phase, while often uncomfortable, is critical; suppressing it prematurely can impair healing.

10
00:01:12,025 --> 00:01:15,450
The proliferation phase begins around day three.

11
00:01:15,450 --> 00:01:23,175
Fibroblasts migrate to the wound and begin depositing collagen, forming granulation tissue that provides structural support.

12
00:01:23,175 --> 00:01:30,487
Angiogenesis, the formation of new blood vessels, supplies oxygen and nutrients to the repairing tissue.

13
00:01:30,487 --> 00:01:37,725
Epithelial cells at the wound edges multiply and migrate across the surface in a process called re-epithelialization.

14
00:01:37,725 --> 00:01:45,287
A study in The Lancet described how growth factors such as VEGF and TGF-beta orchestrate these events with remarkable precision.

15
00:01:45,287 --> 00:01:49,187
The final remodeling phase can last months to years.

16
00:01:49,187 --> 00:01:56,375
The initially disorganized collagen fibers are gradually reorganized and cross-linked, increasing tensile strength.

17
00:01:56,375 --> 00:02:00,975
Healed skin typically reaches only 80 percent of its original strength.

18
00:02:00,975 --> 00:02:07,912
